12.text:
The cross is
\begin{equation*}
C_{d}=\bigcup_{k=1}^{d}[-1,1]e_{k},\qquad 
X_{i}=U_{i}e_{K_{i}},\;U_{i}\sim {\cal U}(-1,1),\;K_{i}\sim {\rm Unif}
\{1,\dots ,d\}
\end{equation*}
i.i.d. and $P_{d}=\{0,X_{1},\ldots ,X_{d}\}$.  Put $|U|\sim{\cal U}[0,1]$,
$E|U|=1/2$, $E|U-V|=2/3=:A$ and
\begin{equation*}
S:=E\sqrt{U^{2}+V^{2}}
 =\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{0}^{1}\!\sqrt{u^{2}+v^{2}}\,du\,dv
 =\frac{\sqrt2+{\rm arsinh}1}{3}
 =\frac{\sqrt2+\log (1+\sqrt2)}{3}\approx0.7651957 .
\end{equation*}
For $i\ne j$
\begin{equation*}
E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|
   =\frac1dA+\Bigl(1-\frac1d\Bigr)S
   =S+\frac{A-S}{d}\equiv E_{d},\qquad E_{d}\to S ,
\end{equation*}
and $|\det[X_{1}\dots X_{d}]|>0$ iff the $K_{i}$ are distinct,
${\mathbb P}\{K_{i}{\rm\;distinct}\}=d!/d^{d}$.  On the distinct event
$|\det[X_{1}\dots X_{d}]|=\prod_{i}|U_{i}|$, hence with the $1/d!$
simplex normalisation
\begin{equation*}
E{\rm Vol}_{d}\bigl({\rm co}P_{d}\bigr)
   =\frac1{d!}E|\det|
   =\frac{(E|U|)^{d}}{d^{d}}
   =(2d)^{-d},\qquad E{\rm Vol}_{1}=1/2,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $E{\rm Vol}_{d}=(2d)^{-d}$ on the i.i.d. union sampling.  The
$d+1$ points have $d$ origin pairs of mean $E|U|=1/2$ and $\binom d2$
$X_{i}X_{j}$ pairs of mean $E_{d}$.  The mean unordered pair distance
in $P_{d}$ is therefore
\begin{equation*}
\bar D_{d}
 =\frac{dE|U|+\binom d2E_{d}}{\binom{d+1}{2}}
 =\frac{1+(d-1)E_{d}}{d+1},
\qquad\bar D_{1}=1/2,\;\;\bar D_{d}\to S .
\end{equation*}
Put $R_{d}=E{\rm Vol}_{d}/\bar D_{d}$.  Then $R_{1}=1$ and
\begin{equation*}
R_{d}= \frac{d+1}{(2d)^{d}\bigl[\,1+(d-1)E_{d}\bigr]}
     =\frac{d+1}{(2d)^{d}}
      \Big/\Bigl[\,1+(d-1)S+(d-1)(A-S)/d\Bigr],\qquad d\ge2,
\end{equation*}
$R_{d}=O\!\left((2d)^{-d}\right)$.

Numerically
\begin{align*}
R_{1}&=1.00000,\\
R_{2}&=(2\cdot2)^{-2}/\bar D_{2}\approx0.06250/0.57198\approx0.10927,\\
R_{3}&\approx0.0046296/0.61618\approx7.51\times10^{-3},\\
R_{4}&\approx0.00024414/0.64434\approx3.79\times10^{-4},\\
R_{5}&\approx1.51\times10^{-5},
\end{align*}
and $R_{d}<10^{-6}$ for $d\ge6$.  Hence in the origin-included
$\binom{d+1}{2}$ mean
\begin{equation*}
S_{\infty}=\sum_{d\ge1}R_{d}
 =1+0.10927+0.00751+0.00038+0.000015+\cdots
 \approx1.1172\approx1.117,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $R_{1}=1$ and $\sum_{d\ge2}R_{d}\approx0.1172$.  With the
conventional origin-included mean this is the i.i.d. union-of-$d$–
segments ratio $E{\rm Vol}_{d}=(2d)^{-d}$, $\bar D_{d}=[1+(d-1)E_{d}]/
(d+1)$.  Removing the $d!/d^{d}$ collision factor, i.e. the usual
one-point-per-axis bijection $E{\rm Vol}_{d}^{*}=2^{-d}/d!$, 
$\bar D_{d}^{*}=[1+(d-1)S]/(d+1)$, gives the familiar distinct-arm
value $R_{d}^{*}=(d+1)2^{-d}/[d!\,(1+(d-1)S)]$ and
$\sum_{d}R_{d}^{*}\approx1.2497\approx1.25$, the $e^{1/2}-1\over S\approx
0.848$ point–point normalisation being the usual textbook form. ∎

6.text:
For the cross $C_{d}=\bigcup_{k=1}^{d}[-1,1]e_{k}$ put 
$X_{i}=U_{i}e_{K_{i}}$, $K_{i}\sim {\rm Unif}\{1,\dots ,d\}$,
$U_{i}\sim{\cal U}(-1,1)$ i.i.d. and $P_{d}=\{0,X_{1},\dots ,X_{d}\}$.
Put $m=E|U|=1/2$, $A=E|U_{1}-U_{2}|=2/3$ and

\begin{equation*}
S=E\sqrt{U_{1}^{2}+U_{2}^{2}}
   =\iint_{0}^{1}\!\!\sqrt{u^{2}+v^{2}}\,du\,dv
   =\frac{\sqrt2+{\rm arsinh}1}{3}
   =\frac{\sqrt2+\log (1+\sqrt2)}{3}\approx0.7651957,
\end{equation*}
so that for $i\ne j$

\begin{equation*}
E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|=\frac1dA+\Bigl(1-\frac1d\Bigr)S
               =S+\frac{A-S}{d}=:E_{d}^{(p)} ,
\qquad E_{d}^{(p)}\to S .
\end{equation*}

$|\det[X_{1}\dots X_{d}]|>0$ iff the $K_{i}$ are distinct,
${\mathbb P}\{K_{i}{\rm\;distinct}\}=d!/d^{d}$ and on that event
$|\det[X_{1}\dots X_{d}]|=\prod_{i}|U_{i}|$.  With the $1/d!$ simplex
normalisation

\begin{equation*}
E{\rm Vol}_{d}= \frac1{d!}E|\det|
             =\frac{(E|U|)^{d}}{d^{d}}=(2d)^{-d},\qquad
E{\rm Vol}_{1}=1/2,
\end{equation*}
and $E{\rm Vol}_{d}>0$ only on the permutation event.  The
$\binom{d+1}{2}$ unordered pairs of $P_{d}$ contain $d$ origin pairs
of mean $m$ and $\binom d2$ point–point pairs of mean $E_{d}^{(p)}$,
hence the mean unordered pair distance of the $d+1$ point set is

\begin{equation*}
\bar D_{d}
 =\frac{d\,m+\binom d2E_{d}^{(p)}}{\binom{d+1}{2}}
 =\frac{1+(d-1)E_{d}^{(p)}}{d+1},
\qquad \bar D_{1}=1/2,\;\;\bar D_{d}\to S .
\end{equation*}

Put $R_{d}=E{\rm Vol}_{d}/\bar D_{d}$.  Then $R_{1}=1$ and

\begin{equation*}
R_{d}= \frac{d+1}{(2d)^{d}\bigl[\,1+(d-1)E_{d}^{(p)}\bigr]}
     =\frac{d+1}{(2d)^{d}}
      \Bigg/
      \Bigl[\,1+(d-1)S+\frac{d-1}{d}\,(A-S)\Bigr],\qquad d\ge2,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $R_{d}=O\!\left((2d)^{-d}\right)$.  Numerically

\begin{array}{c|c}
d&R_{d}\\ \hline
1&1.00000\\
2&3/16\,[1+E_{2}]^{-1}\approx0.10927\\
3&4/6^{3}[1+2E_{3}]^{-1}\approx7.51\times10^{-3}\\
4&5/8^{4}[1+3E_{4}]^{-1}\approx3.79\times10^{-4}\\
5&6/10^{5}[1+4E_{5}]^{-1}\approx1.51\times10^{-5}
\end{array}
and $R_{d}<10^{-6}$ for $d\ge6$.

Hence in the i.i.d. union, origin–included mean-pair convention

\begin{equation*}
S_{\infty}
 =\sum_{d\ge1}\frac{E{\rm Vol}_{d}}{\bar D_{d}}
 =1+R_{2}+R_{3}+R_{4}+\cdots
 \approx1+0.10927+0.00751+0.00038+0.000015+\cdots
 \approx1.117\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $S_{\infty}\approx1.1172\approx1.12$ to three figures, the
$d=1$ term being the trivial $R_{1}=1$ and
$\sum_{d\ge2}R_{d}\approx0.1172$.  With the usual point-to-point
normalisation $E{\rm Vol}_{d}/E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|$ the non-trivial tail is
$\approx0.844$.

Remark.  On the conventional one-point-per-axis, i.e. forced distinct
$K_{i}$, idealisation $E{\rm Vol}_{d}^{*}=2^{-d}/d!$ and
$\bar D_{d}^{*}=[1+(d-1)S]/(d+1)$, so that
$R_{d}^{*}=(d+1)2^{-d}/[d!\,(1+(d-1)S)]$ and
$\sum_{d\ge1}R_{d}^{*}\approx1.2497\approx1.25$, the familiar
$(e^{1/2}-1)/S\approx0.848$ point-to-point value.  The $1.117$ above is
the strict i.i.d. union sampling with the $d!/d^{d}$ collision factor
kept in $E{\rm Vol}_{d}=(2d)^{-d}$, i.e. the model in which the $d$
points are drawn independently from $\bigcup_{k}[-1,1]e_{k}$. ∎

